      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Base rate/reference class: using recent first-print monthly LFS employment changes for the same seasonally adjusted Canada series gives Oct 2025 through May 2026 values of +67, +54, +8.2, -25, -84, +14, -18, and +88 thousand. The simple mean is about +13 thousand, a reasonable outside-view prior for a noisy monthly employment-change target."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Current-release update: May's +88,000 rebound and lower 6.6% unemployment argue against extrapolating the February-April weakness directly. But the May report also said the job-finding rate remained below its 2017-2019 average and that wholesale/retail and manufacturing had soft spots, so I only move modestly above the +13 thousand base rate; the June services/event-demand factor is kept as a small qualitative adjustment rather than a large driver."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: persistence/base-rate model on recent same-variant first prints (+67, +54, +8.2, -25, -84, +14, -18, +88) gives mean = 104.2/8 = 13.0 thousand; adjustment components are +8 for May momentum and small services/event demand, -3 for elevated unemployment and weak job-finding, giving point = 18 thousand. For a change/flow series, sigma is computed from the values themselves: sigma = 56.1 thousand using the sample standard deviation; 80% half-width is roughly 1.28*sigma = 1.28*56.1 = 71.8 thousand, rounded to 72. Final interval is 18 - 72 = -54 and 18 + 72 = 90 thousand."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Counter-considerations: upside risk is a stronger services and youth hiring month, possibly helped by June travel/event activity, which would land above the interval if gains exceed about +90,000. Downside risk is renewed private-sector weakness, tariff-sensitive manufacturing cuts, or reversal after May's full-time surge, which would land below the interval if losses exceed about -54,000."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Review disposition: accepted the reviewer suggestion to make the no-leakage boundary explicit and to keep the services/event-demand factor as only a small qualitative adjustment; no blocking fixes were required, and the resolver, base-rate, sigma-based interval, and ledger URL discrepancy treatment remain unchanged."
      },
